= BITwiki Foundational Triad =

BITwiki uses three complementary questions as a persistent design lens.

== Ontology — what exists? ==
Ontology concerns entities, classes, structures, relationships, and the distinctions needed to describe what a thing is.

In BITwiki this informs entity types, semantic properties, relationships, domains, and the structure of the knowledge graph.

== Epistemology — how do we know? ==
Epistemology concerns sources, evidence, justification, verification, confidence, uncertainty, disagreement, and the conditions under which claims should be accepted or revised.

In BITwiki this informs citations, provenance, epistemic status, uncertainty markers, validation, and review.

== Axiology — what matters? ==
Axiology concerns values, priorities, ethics, invariants, and the standards by which actions or designs are judged.

In BITwiki this primarily informs governance, editorial policy, human agency, privacy, quality standards, and system constraints.

== Rule ==
The triad is a reasoning framework, not a requirement to force every page into three category trees.

